{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "tr-08-advanced-mixed-train-puzzles",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Advanced Mixed Train Puzzles and Shortcuts",
    "description": "Combine crossing models to solve ratio, scaling, bogie, platform, and multi-condition train puzzles efficiently.",
    "teachingApproach": "Teach ratio and cancellation shortcuts: time ratios, platform/train multiples, equal-length pole times, bogie scaling, chained train-plus-platform problems, same/opposite crossing-time ratios, and a changed-length capstone.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 20, Problems on Trains. Approved scope: Teach higher-transfer patterns from the objective exercise: crossing-time ratios at the same speed, platform length as a multiple of train length, equal-length trains whose pole times predict their mutual crossing time, bogie-count changes that scale train length and crossing time, a train crossing another train and then a platform, and chained problems where a pole/platform observation determines train A before it meets train B. Encourage learners to cancel common train lengths or common speeds before calculating and to ask what distance the front must gain for the event to be complete."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Hard train questions often look like they need more formulas. Usually they need fewer calculations.\n\nIf the same speed, same length, or same pair of trains appears twice, look for a ratio or cancellation before touching the numbers.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Crossing times can reveal length ratios without finding speed. At one fixed speed, distance and time scale together.\n\nIf a train crosses a pole in 20 s and a bridge in 50 s, the bridge event covers 50/20 = 2.5 train-lengths in total.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Total bridge-crossing distance = train + bridge = 2.5L.\nSo bridge = 1.5L.\n\nRatio thinking can replace arithmetic when the speed is identical in both events.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
      "id": "q_1",
      "type": "CHOICE",
      "content": {
        "text": "A train crosses a pole in 20 s and a bridge in 50 s at the same speed. Bridge length is how many times the train length?",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "input": {
        "type": "CHOICE",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "a",
            "label": "1.5 times"
          },
          {
            "id": "b",
            "label": "2 times"
          },
          {
            "id": "c",
            "label": "2.5 times"
          },
          {
            "id": "d",
            "label": "3 times"
          }
        ]
      },
